      "created_at": "2019-06-17T19:54:17Z",
      "updated_at": "2019-06-17T19:54:17Z",
      "body": "Thanks for the input @ClearLakeHABMap \r\n\r\nApparently a level of 4 would already be giving out a precision of around 100m ... which is well, quite precise in a personal scale, I think.\r\n\r\nThe function is not a randomiser, because it was deemed important that points in the map would appear stable, and storing randomised coordinates separately seemed... inconvenient. The function pretty much just snaps the coordinates to the nearest intersection point in a grid, drawn accordingly to the precision level. I'm assuming the estimated precision that is shown in Settings > General is calculated assuming locations around the equator, but not quite sure.\r\n\r\nThis is a pretty crude approach, better approaches (in the shape of issues and/or pull requests of implementations :) ) are more than welcome. I think you are right that this \"one size fit all\" approach may not work well for deployments that have scopes covering areas with mixed population densities.. such as a lot of counties, states, as well as any country or continent. Dynamic approaches to solve this sound appealing.\r\n\r\nI am guessing it's a desirable trait to have the markers in the map appear stable as the map gets reloaded, or more markers are added.... which may make dynamic implementations harder. But curious what other people think about this.  \r\n",
      "author_association": "MEMBER",
      "pin": null,
